shading represents the count of responses (darker = more responses).
Categories with zero responses are shown (\code{n = 0}), since finding such
gaps is a main purpose of the plot, especially before DIF analyses,
where a sparse category within a group can stop the model from being
fitted in that group.

When \code{group} is supplied, percentages and the highlight cutoff are
applied within each group, so a cell labelled "5" in the group-A
facet contains the count for group A only.

The caption gives the sample size in the form
\verb{n = X of Y respondents (policy)}. Rows with a missing group are dropped
(counted in \code{Y} only), while missing responses are kept, and each cell
counts the responses to that item.
